## Anoat Expanse

The Anoat Expanse is a remote region of the Outer Rim located far from the densely populated galactic center. Its hyperspace lanes pass through cold, poorly charted systems separated by large areas of empty space. Navigation beacons are uncommon outside the main routes, and abandoned military depots, mining stations, and communication relays can be found throughout the region.

The most notable worlds are Bespin, Hoth, and Anoat. Bespin is the region’s principal commercial center because of its tibanna gas industry and the landing facilities of Cloud City. Hoth has no major civilian population but attracts salvagers, explorers, and groups searching for abandoned military equipment. Anoat itself once served as an industrial and administrative world, although pollution and political decline reduced its importance.

The Expanse has no universally recognized capital. Cloud City functions as its main neutral trade hub, while individual systems are governed by local administrations, mining corporations, or military authorities. Imperial salvage teams and private contractors remain active around former bases. Overall stability is low to moderate, and danger is high beyond protected trade corridors due to severe environments, poor communications, raiders, and unexploded military technology.

## Core Worlds

The Core Worlds occupy the central and most densely developed part of the galaxy. The region contains ancient hyperspace routes, heavily populated systems, powerful governments, major financial institutions, universities, shipyards, and some of the oldest continuously inhabited planets. Travel infrastructure is extensive, although access to political, military, and corporate zones is strictly controlled.

Coruscant is the most important world in the region and traditionally serves as the political capital of the galaxy. Corellia is a major center of transportation, engineering, and starship construction, while Kuat is dominated by military and industrial shipyards. Numerous smaller Core systems contain banking centers, diplomatic stations, luxury worlds, research facilities, and administrative headquarters.

Control of the Core changes with the dominant galactic government, but local planetary authorities and corporations retain significant influence. The region is generally stable and well protected, with frequent patrols and reliable communications. Open piracy is rare near major systems, but political investigations, corporate espionage, surveillance, and jurisdictional conflicts create a different form of risk. Guild operations in the Core usually require legal documentation, discretion, and careful attention to local regulations.

## Corellian Trade Spine

The Corellian Trade Spine is one of the galaxy’s oldest and most important hyperspace corridors. It begins near Corellia and extends from the Core toward the southern and western regions of the galaxy, linking industrial systems with distant markets. Thousands of cargo ships, passenger liners, military transports, and independent freighters travel along its established lanes.

Corellia is the route’s largest commercial and administrative hub. Its shipyards, spaceports, warehouses, and transport companies support traffic moving in every direction. Farther from the Core, the route passes through regional junctions, refueling stations, customs platforms, and cargo depots that connect with worlds such as Bespin and the systems of the Anoat region. Many settlements along the Spine exist almost entirely to service passing vessels.

No single government controls the entire corridor. Core authorities regulate the inner sections, while corporations, local governments, and independent station operators control more distant segments. Stability is high near Corellia but decreases toward the Outer Rim. Smuggling, cargo theft, false distress signals, and attacks near isolated junctions remain common. The route is usually considered moderate risk, although valuable convoys may require armed escort.

## Corporate Sector

The Corporate Sector is a distant region of the Outer Rim administered primarily for commercial development. Its systems contain mineral deposits, factory worlds, company settlements, private research facilities, and large transport networks designed to move resources toward galactic markets. Many planets were developed according to corporate needs rather than the interests of their local populations.

Etti IV serves as the traditional administrative capital, containing executive offices, financial institutions, and the headquarters of the Corporate Sector Authority. Bonadan is one of the region’s busiest industrial and transportation worlds, known for factories, spaceports, cargo terminals, and heavy pollution. Other systems contain mining colonies, agricultural concessions, prison facilities, and privately owned settlements.

The Corporate Sector Authority combines government, police, customs, and commercial regulation within a single organization. Local laws strongly protect company property, trade secrets, production quotas, and transport contracts. Weapons, communications equipment, and private vessels may be inspected without warning. Major commercial zones are stable, but restricted facilities and remote colonies are considerably more dangerous. Guild hunters must verify whether a contract is legal under corporate law or merely authorized by a competing company.

## Hutt Space

Hutt Space is an autonomous region located along the eastern approaches of the Outer Rim. It contains ancient trade routes, wealthy criminal territories, industrial systems, and numerous worlds that operate outside the direct authority of major galactic governments. The region is commercially active but politically fragmented, with influence determined by wealth, family connections, and control of transportation.

Nal Hutta is the traditional political center and homeworld of the most powerful Hutt families. Its moon Nar Shaddaa is the region’s largest commercial hub, filled with spaceports, markets, casinos, warehouses, workshops, and criminal headquarters. Tatooine lies outside the region’s central systems but has frequently remained under strong Hutt economic and political influence. Smaller worlds and stations often depend on Hutt protection or trade agreements.

There is no unified legal code applied equally throughout Hutt Space. Individual kajidics control their own territories and enforce agreements through agents, mercenaries, bounty hunters, and local officials. Business can be predictable when contracts and tribute are respected, but disputes between Hutt families can rapidly destabilize entire systems. Danger is high due to organized crime, slavery, smuggling, piracy, and shifting territorial claims, although established trade centers are usually safe for those with proper permission.

## Kuat Industrial Ring

The Kuat Industrial Ring is a Guild designation for the heavily developed region surrounding Kuat and its associated shipbuilding infrastructure. It lies within the Core and is connected to major military and commercial hyperspace routes. The area contains shipyards, orbital factories, research stations, supply depots, worker habitats, and defensive installations.

Kuat is the dominant planet and the sector’s formal administrative center, while the enormous Kuat Drive Yards ring acts as its true economic capital. Smaller moons, orbital stations, and nearby systems support the shipyards by producing armor, engines, electronics, fuel, and construction materials. Most settlements exist to serve industrial production or the transportation of completed vessels.

The sector is controlled by Kuat’s government, major shipbuilding corporations, and whichever galactic military currently protects the yards. Licensed traffic is closely monitored through checkpoints, transponder verification, cargo inspections, and restricted flight corridors. Stability is very high within authorized areas, but violations are treated severely. The principal dangers are industrial accidents, military security responses, corporate espionage, sabotage, and attempts to steal prototype technology.

## Mandalore Sector

The Mandalore Sector lies in the Outer Rim and contains the traditional territories of Mandalorian clans and houses. Its systems include dry terrestrial worlds, forested planets, moons, mining colonies, and fortified settlements. Centuries of warfare have left the region with abandoned fortresses, ruined cities, hidden weapon stores, and large areas of damaged infrastructure.

Mandalore is the cultural and political center of the sector. Sundari serves as its best-known capital, while Keldabe remains an important historical and industrial city. Concordia is closely connected to mining and warrior traditions, while Kalevala and Krownest are associated with influential Mandalorian houses. Smaller clan territories may include entire moons, remote strongholds, or isolated settlements.

Control of the sector depends on the strength of its ruling clans. A recognized Mand’alor or central government may unite the region, but periods of division produce competing authorities and territorial disputes. Local laws are shaped by clan tradition, military obligations, inheritance, and personal oaths. Stability ranges from moderate to very low. Danger is high because many inhabitants are heavily armed, old conflicts remain unresolved, and outside governments frequently attempt to influence the sector.

## Outer Rim Frontier

The Outer Rim Frontier is a broad Guild operational region covering scattered systems beyond the reliable protection of major galactic governments. It includes remote trade worlds, agricultural planets, former colonies, military ruins, and settlements separated by long stretches of poorly monitored space. Infrastructure varies greatly, and many routes depend on local navigation data rather than official beacons.

Notable worlds include Tatooine, Lothal, Nevarro, Ord Mantell, Ryloth, Mon Cala, and Mustafar. Lothal and Ryloth support established populations and regional governments, while Nevarro and Tatooine function as frontier trade centers. Ord Mantell is known for salvage, industry, and criminal activity. Mon Cala is a major aquatic civilization and shipbuilding world, whereas Mustafar is valued primarily for mining, military installations, and specialized resources.

The Frontier has no single capital or controlling faction. Each system follows its own government, clan authority, corporate administration, or criminal arrangement. Regional hubs such as Lothal Capital City, Mos Eisley, Nevarro City, and Lessu provide trade and limited administrative services. Stability is generally low, and danger ranges from moderate near developed planets to extreme in isolated systems. Piracy, raiders, hostile wildlife, political conflict, and delayed emergency response are common problems.

## Shadow Nebula

The Shadow Nebula is a Guild-defined navigation zone located beyond the most reliable Outer Rim routes. It is surrounded by dark stellar clouds, sensor interference, gravitational disturbances, and systems that remain absent from standard commercial charts. Conventional navigation is difficult, and vessels often require local coordinates or experienced guides to pass safely through the region.

Dathomir is the most prominent inhabited world associated with the zone. Its forests, swamps, mountains, clan territories, and ancient ruins attract explorers, collectors, cults, and researchers despite the danger. The surrounding systems contain dead worlds, abandoned observation posts, hidden sanctuaries, and unregistered settlements. There is no conventional sector capital; the Nightsister fortress and other clan strongholds serve as the nearest equivalents to centers of authority.

No galactic government maintains effective control over the Shadow Nebula. Dathomirian clans govern their own territories, while smugglers, fugitives, and secret organizations use isolated systems as hiding places. Stability is extremely low and reliable information is scarce. The region is considered extreme risk because of navigation failures, aggressive wildlife, unknown ruins, hostile local groups, unusual Force phenomena, and the near absence of rescue services.

## Spice Run

The Spice Run is a Guild designation for the network of hyperspace routes used to transport spice, medical compounds, stimulants, and restricted substances between producing worlds and major black-market centers. The corridor crosses sections of the Outer Rim and connects industrial mining systems with Hutt-controlled trade routes and independent frontier ports.

Kessel is the route’s principal production center, while Ryloth contributes valuable spice and serves as an important regional supplier. Nal Hutta and Nar Shaddaa are major distribution and financial hubs. Tatooine provides remote landing sites, repair facilities, and connections to smugglers operating outside formal customs networks. Numerous refueling stations, hidden depots, and transfer points exist between the major systems.

The Spice Run has no official capital. Nar Shaddaa acts as its commercial center, while Kessel remains its most strategically important source world. Control is divided among Hutt kajidics, Pyke operations, local governments, mining authorities, and independent smuggling groups. Danger is consistently high because shipments attract pirates, rival syndicates, corrupt officials, and military patrols. Even legal cargo may become involved in territorial disputes or be mistaken for contraband.
